 "backlog": [
  {
   "alpha": 1,
   "beta": 2,
   "family": "data",
   "id": "b028",
   "spec": "Append the 167-bit MACCS structural keys as an extra block: from rdkit.Chem import MACCSkeys, then for each molecule use np.array(MACCSkeys.GenMACCSKeys(mol), dtype=np.float32) (or GetFingerprintAsNumPy on the returned ExplicitBitVect). Compute it inside featurize alongside the Morgan fingerprints and hstack it after the existing fingerprint columns and before the descriptor block. Because base_predict slices descriptors as Xa[:, 2*N_BITS:], update that slice index to 2*N_BITS + 167 so the descriptors are still the block passed to the RBF kernel. The Tanimoto kernel should remain on the ECFP bits only.",
   "status": "done",
   "times_tried": 1,
   "title": "Add MACCS keys to the feature matrix"
  },
  {
   "alpha": 1,
   "beta": 1,
   "family": "data",
   "id": "b036",
   "spec": "Use calculated logP as a physics prior. In fit_predict, compute c = Crippen.MolLogP for each train and test molecule (from rdkit.Chem import Crippen; cache per SMILES like the descriptors). Fit a 1-D Huber regression (sklearn HuberRegressor) of y on c over the training set to get a, b, and set the baseline to base = a*c + b. Run the whole existing pipeline (OOF stacking, base_predict, meta) on the residual target r = y - base_tr instead of y, then return meta.predict(base_te) + base_te_prior, where base_te_prior = a*c_te + b. Features are unchanged.",
   "status": "open",
   "times_tried": 1,
   "title": "Residual learning on Crippen logP"
  },
  {
   "alpha": 1,
   "beta": 2,
   "family": "architecture",
   "id": "b044",
   "spec": "In base_predict, add a fourth base learner: catboost.CatBoostRegressor(iterations=800, learning_rate=0.05, depth=6, loss_function='MAE', rsm=0.2, random_seed=seed, thread_count=8, verbose=0), fit on Xa, ya and predict Xb. Append p_cb as a fourth column of the returned matrix, and change oof = np.zeros((len(y), 3)) to np.zeros((len(y), 4)). The positive Ridge meta-learner stays. If runtime gets near the limit, reduce iterations to 500.",
   "status": "done",
   "times_tried": 1,
   "title": "Add CatBoost as fourth base learner"
  },
  {
   "alpha": 1,
   "beta": 2,
   "family": "optimizer",
   "id": "b052",
   "spec": "Change the LightGBM objective from regression_l1 to huber with alpha (the delta parameter) set to about 0.5, since logD targets have a range of roughly -1.5 to 4.5 and outliers are common. In lgb_params, set objective='huber' and add alpha=0.5. Huber gives smooth gradients near zero and robustness to outliers. Keep all other settings the same. Also try a second variant with objective='fair' and fair_c=0.5 if the first is not clearly better, but ship only the better one.",
   "status": "done",
   "times_tried": 1,
   "title": "Huber objective for the LightGBM learner"
  },
  {
   "alpha": 1,
   "beta": 2,
   "family": "data",
   "id": "b060",
   "spec": "Change the settings to N_BITS = 2048 and RADIUS = 3. Since the code slices descriptors as Xa[:, 2*N_BITS:] and the kernel uses Ftr[:, :N_BITS], those slices update automatically. Larger substructures and fewer bit collisions should help both the Tanimoto KRR and the trees. Make no other change. If runtime is too long, lower RF n_estimators from 200 to 150.",
   "status": "done",
   "times_tried": 1,
   "title": "Radius-3 ECFP with 2048 bits"
  }
 ],
